A||N||6||007DOUG||Parking brake.....outta ideas||||1||
Z||000000||007DOUG||10-07-2000||09:04 PM||||I've installed new rear calipers, all new brake cables, and new pads. <P>I've adjusted the p-brake to its max, and still no holding power. It barely engages.<P>Now what?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||10-07-2000||09:17 PM||||I just finished putting new rotors and pads on my car and am in the same situation- no matter what I do the parking brake won't hold. The handle comes up twice as high as before. I hope somebody knows!||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||10-07-2000||09:24 PM||||I had the exact same thing happen a couple of weeks ago. The front cable is too long. I looked around for something that had an o.d. that will fit inside of the equalizer and an i.d. that the e-brake front cable will go through. <P>You put the end of the e-brake front into the<BR>spacer described above and through it. You then put the spacer and front cable end through the equalizer where the front cable formerly was by itself. Now instead of the front cable end sticking through the equalizer, only the cable inside of the cable housing sticks through. <P>When I did this and adjusted the cable adjuster so that I could lock the  wheels, I had about 1/2 inch of adjustment left.<P>I used a reducer fitting that had come with a mechanical temperature gage. It was about 3/4 inch long. I cut the hex off it with a hacksaw. <P>I could probably adjust it tighter but now it holds on a hill if I also leave the car in gear (it's a 4 speed).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT-maniac||10-08-2000||02:38 AM||||Well that's strange because I replaced the parking cable on my car with the stock one and didn't find it too long at all. It appeared to be too long at first until I realized that I was missing a spring on one side. There's supposed to be springs around the lever on each side that hold the levers away from the disc when the brake is disengaged. Maybe you should check to make sure you have both springs||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierodave||10-08-2000||04:08 AM||||Doug  you have the e-brake arm on the caliper in the wrong position!<BR>the arm can't swing enough to engage the e-brake cabels  if when the cable is off that side caliper and you can move the arm more than a 1/2 inch before it clamps the rotor  you need to remove the arm and turn it ahead one cog!<BR>FD<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.theglobe.com/fierodave/gifs/fierosig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||007DOUG||10-08-2000||09:56 AM||||FD: So in other words, make adjustment at the caliper.<P>I've got the arm as far "north" as it'll go. I'll try removing it, give it a crank or two, and see what happens.<P>GT maniac: Got both springs, thats what makes it release.<P>thanx, Doug||||reg||1
Z||000006||William Federle||10-08-2000||11:34 AM||||I don't want to beat this to death, but I put the spring that holds the lever against the stop back on both the driver and passenger side. The cable was still too long.<P>One thing - I did notice that the thing the front cable attaches to at the handle can swivel to point to the front or to the rear. It looks like it was designed to point to the rear - that way it retains the cable inside of it. The front cable I removed was attached to it with it (the swivel) pointed to the front. I tried to attach the new cable that way but it wasn't long enough.<BR>||||reg||1
